10:46:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: An oven is a relatively small, sealed, insulated box with a powerful heat source. Given enough time, most of the air and objects inside get close to the set temperature. A house is different because it is being heated **while continuously losing heat** to the winter outdoors. It reaches a steady state, not perfect equilibrium: - The furnace adds heat to indoor air. - Heat continuously flows through walls, windows, ceilings, and floors toward the cold outdoors. - Therefore there must be a temperature gradient: the indoor-facing surface of a window or exterior wall can be cooler than the room air, because heat is passing through it. For example, with 72°F indoor air and 20°F outside air, the inside surface of an ordinary window might be in the 50s or low 60s—not 72°F. You radiate heat toward that cooler surface, so you feel chilly near it even though the thermostat says 72. If a house were perfectly insulated and sealed, had evenly distributed heating, and enough time to equilibrate, then yes: walls, furniture, air, etc. would all settle near 72°F and it would feel essentially the same in winter as in summer. Real houses never get that condition because winter heat loss is ongoing.
