10:46:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your HVAC guy is basically right in the practical sense: a well-sealed, well-insulated house with properly designed heating should have **even room temperatures** and should not leave you feeling noticeably cold near walls or windows. “One even temperature” means the house is comfortable and has little room-to-room or surface-to-air variation—not that every layer of glass, wall, and insulation is exactly 72.0°F. The key distinction is: - **Good insulation and good windows make interior surfaces close to room temperature.** - They cannot make them *exactly* room temperature while it is colder outside, because some heat is still flowing outward. But the remaining difference may be only a few degrees and often isn’t noticeable. For example, in a high-performance house at 72°F indoors, an interior wall surface may effectively be 71–72°F, and a good window’s inside surface might be upper 60s even on a cold day. That is very different from a drafty old house with cold glass and walls. So if your house is truly evenly heated, well insulated, has no drafts, and its surfaces are near 72°F, then **72°F should feel essentially the same in winter as it does in summer.** There is no special physical rule that makes identical indoor conditions feel colder merely because it is winter outside. When people report that it does, likely explanations are smaller practical differences—lower surface temperatures, airflow, cold floors, thermostat location—or ordinary perception and clothing habits. But your objection is valid: in a properly conditioned house, those effects should be greatly reduced.
